WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:03.850
Some say there has never been a chess player
as great as Bobby Fischer.

00:00:03.850 --> 00:00:08.559
A leading Russian opponent described him as
“an achilles without an achilles heel”.

00:00:08.559 --> 00:00:13.209
Fischer was not only known for being
a chess genius but also for spiraling into

00:00:13.209 --> 00:00:19.123
insanity. This video will take you through
his highs and his lows.

00:00:19.123 --> 00:00:24.121
Bobby Fischer was born in Chicago on March
9, 1943 and raised in Brooklyn by a single

00:00:24.121 --> 00:00:26.750
mother from Switzerland born to Jewish parents.

00:00:26.750 --> 00:00:28.100
They were poor.

00:00:28.100 --> 00:00:31.789
Regina Fischer didn’t even have the money
to patch up Bobby's torn shoes when he was

00:00:31.789 --> 00:00:32.820
a kid.

00:00:32.820 --> 00:00:36.380
Regina was divorced from Hans Gerhardt Fischer,
a German biophysicist.

00:00:36.380 --> 00:00:41.660
But Bobby’s actual father was most likely
the result of an affair with her friend Paul

00:00:41.660 --> 00:00:42.660
Nemenyi.

00:00:42.660 --> 00:00:44.920
A Hungarian-Jewish physicist.

00:00:44.920 --> 00:00:48.930
When Bobby was six, his sister Joanne bought
him a chess set and taught him to play.

00:00:48.930 --> 00:00:53.120
He felt chess was more exciting than Monopoly
because no luck was involved.

00:00:53.120 --> 00:00:55.059
And he found it far more challenging.

00:00:55.059 --> 00:00:58.969
His sister soon grew tired of the game, and
his mother was too busy working as a teacher

00:00:58.969 --> 00:01:00.020
and a nurse.

00:01:00.020 --> 00:01:04.430
So, Bobby spent several hours every day playing
against…himself.

00:01:04.430 --> 00:01:08.880
He loved the thousands of possible moves and
the complex strategy involved.

00:01:08.880 --> 00:01:12.130
After a year of this, his mother took him
to the Brooklyn Chess Club where he had lessons

00:01:12.130 --> 00:01:16.950
a couple of times a week from one of the club’s
best players, Carmine Nigro.

00:01:16.950 --> 00:01:18.990
Nigh-grow charged him a dollar an hour.

00:01:18.990 --> 00:01:22.229
But Bobby believes he wasn’t in it for the
money but simply to make sure he took the

00:01:22.229 --> 00:01:23.909
lessons seriously.

00:01:23.909 --> 00:01:25.319
And apparently, he did.

00:01:25.319 --> 00:01:27.770
He was utterly obsessed with chess.

00:01:27.770 --> 00:01:30.829
One might even say it was his best friend.

00:01:30.829 --> 00:01:33.459
Fischer didn’t have any real friends growing
up.

00:01:33.459 --> 00:01:37.109
He thought having friends was only important
for kids who had secrets and wanted someone

00:01:37.109 --> 00:01:38.499
to share them with.

00:01:38.499 --> 00:01:42.469
He had no secrets so, apparently, he didn’t
feel the need to have friends.

00:01:42.469 --> 00:01:45.669
Fischer would spend Sundays playing chess
with Nigro (Nigh-grow) in Manhattan’s Washington

00:01:45.669 --> 00:01:46.859
Square Park.

00:01:46.859 --> 00:01:51.139
He began spending less time with Nigro when
he started being tutored by Jack Collins,

00:01:51.139 --> 00:01:55.039
a master player who had once ranked as high
as #17 in the U.S.

00:01:55.039 --> 00:01:56.719
Collins spent his life in a wheelchair.

00:01:56.719 --> 00:01:59.349
He never quite fit in, just like Fischer.

00:01:59.349 --> 00:02:01.819
He was careful not to say he was Fischer’s
coach.

00:02:01.819 --> 00:02:06.709
That would be like saying someone had to coach
Beethoven, Shakespeare, or Leonardo da Vinci.

00:02:06.709 --> 00:02:10.819
He thought Fischer was a genius on the level
of those geniuses.

00:02:10.819 --> 00:02:16.110
Collins did have an extensive library collection
of chess books which Fischer devoured - spending

00:02:16.110 --> 00:02:19.360
five to ten hours a day reading and studying.

00:02:19.360 --> 00:02:23.980
He would need the preparation when he faced
older and more experienced opponents.

00:02:23.980 --> 00:02:29.360
When Fischer was 13 years old, he faced 26-year-old
Donald Byrne, one of the top ten players in

00:02:29.360 --> 00:02:30.360
America.

00:02:30.360 --> 00:02:32.769
Fischer played in black, Byrne in white.

00:02:32.769 --> 00:02:36.200
The “Game of the Century” - as it’s
remembered - took place in New York City on

00:02:36.200 --> 00:02:38.650
October 17, 1956.

00:02:38.650 --> 00:02:41.909
Fischer’s moves looked like lethal art.

00:02:41.909 --> 00:02:44.340
He even dared to sacrifice his queen.

00:02:44.340 --> 00:02:47.460
Fischer got a thrill out of breaking his opponent’s
ego.

00:02:47.460 --> 00:02:50.049
His opponent was outclassed and outmatched.

00:02:50.049 --> 00:02:52.739
The game put Fischer on the map.

00:02:52.739 --> 00:02:55.560
The world knew it had a genius on its hands.

00:02:55.560 --> 00:03:00.530
The following year, when he was still only
14 years old, he won the U.S. Championship.

00:03:00.530 --> 00:03:04.069
A year later, he became the youngest ever
grandmaster.

00:03:04.069 --> 00:03:10.630
And in 1972, he defeated the Soviet Union’s
Boris Spassky to become world champion.

00:03:10.630 --> 00:03:14.469
This was stunning: the Soviets lived and breathed
chess.

00:03:14.469 --> 00:03:19.719
They were more obsessed than Canadians are
about hockey, or the Chinese about table tennis.

00:03:19.719 --> 00:03:23.959
Then comes along a lone wolf from America
who was up against the entire Soviet chess

00:03:23.959 --> 00:03:24.959
institution.

00:03:24.959 --> 00:03:27.920
It was a David versus Goliath matchup.

00:03:27.920 --> 00:03:32.109
Bobby Fischer singlehandedly beat the Soviets
at their own game.

00:03:32.109 --> 00:03:36.159
This took place during the Cold War and the
symbolism wasn’t lost on anyone.

00:03:36.159 --> 00:03:40.139
The world’s two superpowers battled it out
on a chess board.

00:03:40.139 --> 00:03:43.450
Except Fischer nearly skipped the “match
of the century”.

00:03:43.450 --> 00:03:47.909
He refused to fly to Reykjavik, Iceland for
the tournament unless he got more money including

00:03:47.909 --> 00:03:51.049
a guarantee of a slice of the lucrative TV
revenue.

00:03:51.049 --> 00:03:57.239
U.S. Secretary of State Henry Kissinger had
to insist he “...get his butt over to Iceland."

00:03:57.239 --> 00:04:01.359
He finally flew there after a British investment
banker offered to double the prize money to

00:04:01.359 --> 00:04:02.849
$250,000.

00:04:02.849 --> 00:04:05.499
Fischer was still in a foul mood when he arrived.

00:04:05.499 --> 00:04:08.280
He played poorly during the first match and
lost.

00:04:08.280 --> 00:04:12.410
He didn’t even show up for the second match,
complaining that the TV cameras distracted

00:04:12.410 --> 00:04:13.410
him.

00:04:13.410 --> 00:04:14.410
So he lost automatically.

00:04:14.410 --> 00:04:18.430
The tournament organizers conceded to demands
for the third match to be played without TV

00:04:18.430 --> 00:04:20.019
cameras in the room.

00:04:20.019 --> 00:04:23.000
From that point on, his playing got stronger.

00:04:23.000 --> 00:04:28.139
Fischer, the 29-year-old who dropped out of
high school, would beat Spassky to become

00:04:28.139 --> 00:04:34.730
World Champion - single-handedly dismantling
the Soviet Union’s 24-year hold on the game.

00:04:34.730 --> 00:04:39.919
He became a hero to millions of Americans
and inspired new interest in the game of chess.

00:04:39.919 --> 00:04:43.510
Some chess clubs saw membership double during
the Fischer frenzy.

00:04:43.510 --> 00:04:46.770
He was a star and made the rounds of American
television.

00:04:46.770 --> 00:04:50.490
On the Carson Tonight show, he showed off
his brilliance by solving a sliding puzzle

00:04:50.490 --> 00:04:52.340
in 17 seconds.

00:04:52.340 --> 00:04:56.580
This was not very well mixed up.
I want to say that it wasn't well mixed up.

00:04:57.685 --> 00:04:58.685
17!

00:04:58.685 --> 00:05:02.296
Well, they didn’t mix it up too well.
Takes about 30 seconds.

00:05:04.250 --> 00:05:10.440
He reportedly had an IQ of 181 which is said
to be even higher than Albert Einstein’s.

00:05:10.440 --> 00:05:13.880
Although he scaled the heights of fame, Fischer
wasn’t all that likable.

00:05:13.880 --> 00:05:17.919
He had a habit of saying controversial things
though people tended to ignore it because

00:05:17.919 --> 00:05:20.889
he was the Cold War chess hero after all.

00:05:20.889 --> 00:05:24.400
This is what he once said about female chess
players in an interview with the Canadian

00:05:24.400 --> 00:05:25.500
Broadcasting Corporation.

00:05:25.500 --> 00:05:32.258
The women aren't really very good players. 
I mean, I could give them a knight and still win easily.

00:05:32.258 --> 00:05:34.726
Why is this. Do women make bad chess players?

00:05:34.726 --> 00:05:37.287
Oh they're terrible chess players.

00:05:37.287 --> 00:05:38.691
I mean, some are better than others, y'know.

00:05:39.657 --> 00:05:42.500
Why do you think so? And they don't
play in men's tournaments.

00:05:42.500 --> 00:05:43.309
I don’t know why.

00:05:43.309 --> 00:05:45.822
I guess they're just not so smart.

00:05:45.822 --> 00:05:49.294
Would you call yourself a misogynist?

00:05:49.294 --> 00:05:51.640
Excuse me, what’s the definition of that
word?

00:05:51.640 --> 00:05:52.199
A woman-hater.

00:05:52.199 --> 00:05:53.199
No.

00:05:53.199 --> 00:05:55.699
No?
What do you think of women?

00:05:55.699 --> 00:05:59.720
When it comes to chess, not too much.

00:05:59.720 --> 00:06:02.230
He managed to offend everyone.

00:06:02.230 --> 00:06:07.069
During an interview with a Philippine radio
station hours after 9/11, he said the attacks

00:06:07.069 --> 00:06:08.840
were “wonderful news.

00:06:08.840 --> 00:06:10.819
I applaud the act.

00:06:10.819 --> 00:06:15.210
The U.S. and Israel have been slaughtering
the Palestinians, just slaughtering them for

00:06:15.210 --> 00:06:16.210
years.

00:06:16.210 --> 00:06:18.539
I want to see the U.S. wiped out.”

00:06:18.539 --> 00:06:23.360
He never hid his hatred of Jews, repeatedly
making anti-Semitic remarks - despite being

00:06:23.360 --> 00:06:24.949
Jewish ethnically.

00:06:24.949 --> 00:06:30.550
His anti-American, anti-Semitic comments coincided
with his growing paranoia.

00:06:30.550 --> 00:06:33.200
He decided the U.S. government was out to
get him.

00:06:33.200 --> 00:06:37.770
He also believed the Israeli national intelligence
agency - the Mossad - was after him.

00:06:37.770 --> 00:06:41.819
He refused to defend his world championship
title in 1975.

00:06:41.819 --> 00:06:46.569
He complained that opponents were trying to
poison his food, his hotel rooms were bugged,

00:06:46.569 --> 00:06:48.680
he feared the Russians wanted to bomb his
plane.

00:06:48.680 --> 00:06:53.360
For years, Fischer carried a blue cardboard
box with him wherever he went and refused

00:06:53.360 --> 00:06:54.659
to say what was inside.

00:06:54.659 --> 00:06:58.870
Once, when he went to use a restaurant restroom,
he left it on the table and the friend he

00:06:58.870 --> 00:07:00.490
was with peaked inside.

00:07:00.490 --> 00:07:02.030
He saw a Bible.

00:07:02.030 --> 00:07:05.400
Religion appealed to his desire for logic
and order.

00:07:05.400 --> 00:07:10.490
Except he later cut ties with the Worldwide
Church of God - funded by a televangelist

00:07:10.490 --> 00:07:14.639
after complaining that Herbert Armstrong’s
teachings made members lose their minds and

00:07:14.639 --> 00:07:15.639
become “zombies”.

00:07:15.639 --> 00:07:16.639
He later cut ties with the organization.

00:07:16.639 --> 00:07:20.699
One of the surest signs of his mental decline
was that he insisted on removing his dental

00:07:20.699 --> 00:07:21.699
fillings!

00:07:21.699 --> 00:07:22.699
a

00:07:22.699 --> 00:07:25.050
He explained that he didn’t want anything
artificial in him and had heard of a guy wounded

00:07:25.050 --> 00:07:31.080
in WWII who had a metal plate in his head
that picked up vibrations and even radio transmissions.

00:07:31.080 --> 00:07:35.210
His mental instability manifested in his physical
appearance.

00:07:35.210 --> 00:07:39.380
Gone was the athletic young man who swam and
played tennis to keep in shape for chess which

00:07:39.380 --> 00:07:42.500
requires enormous mental and physical endurance.

00:07:42.500 --> 00:07:44.710
He was unrecognizable.

00:07:44.710 --> 00:07:48.780
California police officers mistakenly arrested
him because he resembled a man who wanted

00:07:48.780 --> 00:07:49.819
for robbery.

00:07:49.819 --> 00:07:54.420
He spent 48 hours in jail and wrote a book
about the alleged torture he endured.

00:07:54.420 --> 00:08:00.650
During his descent into madness, Fischer didn’t
play a competitive game in public for twenty

00:08:00.650 --> 00:08:01.840
year.

00:08:01.840 --> 00:08:03.180
But eventually, he had to.

00:08:03.180 --> 00:08:05.050
He had run out of money.

00:08:05.050 --> 00:08:09.789
In 1992, he agreed to a $5 million dollar
rematch against his Russian arch-rival (arch)

00:08:09.789 --> 00:08:11.469
Spassky in Yugoslavia.

00:08:11.469 --> 00:08:14.759
Except the U.S. government wasn’t too pleased
about that.

00:08:14.759 --> 00:08:18.090
It defied UN sanctions against the war-torn
country.

00:08:18.090 --> 00:08:21.360
Americans were forbidden from doing any business
in Yugoslavia.

00:08:21.360 --> 00:08:25.270
Fischer was warned he’d face up to ten years
in prison if he went ahead with his plans.

00:08:25.270 --> 00:08:26.419
But he didn’t care

00:08:26.419 --> 00:08:28.259
He won the tournament.

00:08:28.259 --> 00:08:32.640
Some believe he was coaxed into playing again
by his teenage Hungarian girlfriend Zita Rajcsanyi

00:08:32.640 --> 00:08:34.160
who was also a chess player.

00:08:34.160 --> 00:08:39.590
She had apparently turned a pen pal relationship
into a romance despite their 30-year age gap.

00:08:39.590 --> 00:08:42.700
Although, Fischer claimed he didn’t have
time for women.

00:08:42.700 --> 00:08:47.610
He is said to have married a Japanese chess
player Miyoko Watai and lived with her in

00:08:47.610 --> 00:08:49.360
Japan for a few years.

00:08:49.360 --> 00:08:53.660
He reportedly hoped that as the spouse of
a Japanese citizen he could stay there as

00:08:53.660 --> 00:08:57.330
he had been on the run from American authorities
for defying sanctions.

00:08:57.330 --> 00:09:00.570
But his attempt to stay in Japan was unsuccessful.

00:09:00.570 --> 00:09:05.020
Authorities arrested him for using a passport
that had been revoked by the U.S. government.

00:09:05.020 --> 00:09:10.320
He begged Iceland to take him in, the country
that made him famous in 1972.

00:09:10.320 --> 00:09:13.000
Iceland welcomed him by making him a citizen.

00:09:13.000 --> 00:09:15.940
He would spend the rest of his days there
living in obscurity.

00:09:15.940 --> 00:09:20.660
So, what caused his spiral into madness?

00:09:20.660 --> 00:09:25.130
Some speculate that training in blindfold
chess can cause mental strain as you can’t

00:09:25.130 --> 00:09:29.190
see or touch the pieces, so you’re forced
to maintain a mental image in your head.

00:09:29.190 --> 00:09:34.290
However, there were signs something was not
right with Bobby Fischer much earlier on.

00:09:34.290 --> 00:09:39.080
Legendary chess grandmaster Mikhail Tal picked
up on it back in the fifties, mocking Fischer

00:09:39.080 --> 00:09:40.980
for being “cuckoo”.

00:09:40.980 --> 00:09:45.520
Another chess grandmaster, Pal Benko, once
remarked: “I am not a psychiatrist, but

00:09:45.520 --> 00:09:47.170
it was obvious he was not normal.

00:09:47.170 --> 00:09:52.320
“I told him, ‘You are paranoid,’ and
he said that paranoids can be right.”

00:09:52.320 --> 00:09:56.570
World-renowned chess grandmaster and psychoanalyst
Dr. Reuben Fine described in his book on the

00:09:56.570 --> 00:10:00.620
chess master that Fischer’s mother had consulted
him because she was concerned her teenage

00:10:00.620 --> 00:10:05.120
son was too obsessed with chess and felt he
needed friends and other hobbies.

00:10:05.120 --> 00:10:09.860
Fine described how, when he “...I started
a conversation at one point about what he

00:10:09.860 --> 00:10:11.100
was doing in school.

00:10:11.100 --> 00:10:15.750
As soon as school was mentioned, he became
furious, screamed, 'You have tricked me,'

00:10:15.750 --> 00:10:17.770
and promptly walked out.

00:10:17.770 --> 00:10:22.930
For years afterward, whenever I met him in
clubs or tournaments he gave me angry looks,

00:10:22.930 --> 00:10:27.230
as though I had done him some immeasurable
harm by trying to get a little closer to him."

00:10:27.230 --> 00:10:31.450
In other words, Fischer reacted in a paranoid
way.

00:10:31.450 --> 00:10:33.500
Perhaps school was a sensitive spot.

00:10:33.500 --> 00:10:36.710
He dropped out at the age of 16 to focus on
chess.

00:10:36.710 --> 00:10:41.280
He was always insecure about his lack of education
and decided to trade his casual wear for suits

00:10:41.280 --> 00:10:44.950
when playing chess in order to look more sophisticated.

00:10:44.950 --> 00:10:46.970
Social skills were not his forte.

00:10:46.970 --> 00:10:51.800
When CBS’ 60 Minutes spent his 29th birthday
with him and surprised him with a cake he

00:10:51.800 --> 00:10:54.040
actually REFUSED it.

00:10:54.040 --> 00:10:57.970
Happy birthday to you.

00:10:57.970 --> 00:11:02.880
You were worrying about this?

00:11:02.880 --> 00:11:04.830
Why were you worrying about it?

00:11:04.830 --> 00:11:10.210
You know, first of all, I don’t eat this
kind of cake.

00:11:10.210 --> 00:11:14.690
Second of all, I don’t go for these kinds
of things.

00:11:14.690 --> 00:11:15.923
Shall we take it away, please?

00:11:15.923 --> 00:11:16.923
Yah.

00:11:16.923 --> 00:11:18.310
It appears paranoia ran in his family.

00:11:18.310 --> 00:11:22.910
The FBI described Fischer’s mother Regina
as mentally unstable in their secret files

00:11:22.910 --> 00:11:23.910
on her.

00:11:23.910 --> 00:11:27.310
They had spied on her since the dawn of the
Cold War in the 1940s.

00:11:27.310 --> 00:11:31.860
The FBI suspected that the left-leaning political
activist was a Communist spy.

00:11:31.860 --> 00:11:34.430
Both she and her then-husband had lived in
Russia.

00:11:34.430 --> 00:11:37.930
The agency was also worried that the Russians
had tried to recruit Bobby.

00:11:37.930 --> 00:11:40.450
Bobby’s relationship with his mother was
strained.

00:11:40.450 --> 00:11:45.020
He resented her for abandoning him when he
was a teen so she could focus on her medical

00:11:45.020 --> 00:11:46.020
career.

00:11:46.020 --> 00:11:50.240
Although they hardly saw each other over the
years, he is said to have become distraught

00:11:50.240 --> 00:11:55.500
after she passed away in 1997 quickly followed
by his sister the following year.

00:11:55.500 --> 00:12:00.460
Some believe their deaths caused him to lose
whatever last grasp he had on reality.

00:12:00.460 --> 00:12:04.240
Bobby’s probable father was also believed
to be paranoid.

00:12:04.240 --> 00:12:06.710
Paul Nemenyi was highly intelligent.

00:12:06.710 --> 00:12:11.390
He even collaborated with Albert Einstein’s
son Hans Albert Einstein who was also a scientist.

00:12:11.390 --> 00:12:17.010
Nemenyi emigrated from Hungary to the U.S.
in 1939 but had trouble adjusting.

00:12:17.010 --> 00:12:21.540
He reportedly walked around with soap in his
pockets, paranoid about getting his hands

00:12:21.540 --> 00:12:22.700
dirty.

00:12:22.700 --> 00:12:26.690
Some speculate Bobby Fischer could have had
schizophrenia or Asperger's, though there

00:12:26.690 --> 00:12:30.710
is no evidence that he was diagnosed with
any mental health disorders.

00:12:30.710 --> 00:12:35.160
It also does not appear that Fischer had any
long-term treatment for mental health issues.

00:12:35.160 --> 00:12:41.550
One can only wonder if his life would have
turned out differently if he did.

00:12:41.550 --> 00:12:46.060
During the last years of his life, he stayed
in close touch with a psychiatrist who headed

00:12:46.060 --> 00:12:48.140
Iceland’s hospital for the criminally insane.

00:12:48.140 --> 00:12:54.040
Dr. Magnus Skulason stayed in hospital with
Fischer as he lay dying of kidney failure.

00:12:54.040 --> 00:13:00.860
Fischer passed away on January 17, 2008 and,
according to Dr. Skulason, his final words

00:13:00.860 --> 00:13:05.580
were: "Nothing is as healing as the human
touch."

00:13:05.580 --> 00:13:10.180
Coming from a man who shied away from social
contact, this was a rather remarkable thing

00:13:10.180 --> 00:13:11.350
to hear.

00:13:11.350 --> 00:13:16.720
It was fitting that having fallen in love
with 64 black and white squares on a chess

00:13:16.720 --> 00:13:21.360
board, Bobby Fischer died when he was 64 years
old.

00:13:21.360 --> 00:13:25.410
He asked to be buried as a Catholic.

00:13:25.410 --> 00:13:28.540
Bobby Fischer once wrote in an article for
the Boy Scouts magazine:

00:13:28.540 --> 00:13:34.950
“With talent, study and a positive attitude,
there is no limit to how far you can go.”

00:13:34.950 --> 00:13:38.090
Yet he failed to live up to his own words.

00:13:38.090 --> 00:13:42.110
If Fischer had stayed in the game longer,
perhaps we would have seen a match-up with

00:13:42.110 --> 00:13:46.420
Gary Kasparov - arguably the two greatest
chess players of all time.

00:13:46.420 --> 00:13:49.040
Though, they were 20 years apart.

00:13:49.040 --> 00:13:51.010
But it would never come to pass.

00:13:51.010 --> 00:13:54.300
The person who finally defeated Bobby Fischer
was sadly himself.

